
  [1mB. Validator + [38;5;153mbindForm[0m
  Same core, plus [38;5;153mbindForm(formEl, schema, onValid)[39m that wires the submit listener, validates, renders messages into
  per-field error elements, and calls [38;5;153monValid(values)[39m.
  - Buys: a new form is one call plus a schema — the most reuse.
  - Costs: it has to invent an error-display contract (required markup, class names) when the HTML has no error elements
    at all today, and we have no second form to validate that guess against. Testing it needs a DOM shim, which pulls
    in a dependency we just agreed not to add.
  - Rules out: little permanently, but a wrong rendering contract is the kind of thing forms get written against and
    then hard to change.

  [1mC. Declarative HTML attributes[0m
  Rules live in markup ([38;5;153mdata-rules="required|minLength:8"[39m); a scanner auto-wires every form on the page.
  - Buys: zero JS per form.
  - Costs: it fights the predicate-function decision — arbitrary rules can't be expressed as an attribute string, so
    you'd end up with both systems. Also the most machinery for the least-known requirements.
  - I'd discard this one.

  [1mRecommendation: A.[0m With no second form in hand, the thing we can't get wrong is the pure core; the thing we'd be
  guessing at is error presentation. A gives full reuse of the part that's genuinely shared and stays silent about the
  part we don't know yet — and if the second form shows us the real error UI, [38;5;153mbindForm[39m drops on top without changing
  anything already written. The one honest tradeoff: A leaves a little glue in each form that B would have removed.
